[[:db/retract 17592186068176 :answer-field/choices 17592186068179] [:db/retract 17592186066832 :answer-field/choices 17592186066834] {:db/id 17592186068763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4dcf3cdc-27a5-54d1-8c1d-7131e7d4a1a0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068604 :question/problem "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac {5}{y}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{5}{y},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac 5 y&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac y 5&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {3y} {5x}\t\t\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068597 :question/problem "What is the result of dividing $\\dfrac 3 x$ by $\\dfrac y {6x^2}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{x} \\div \\dfrac{y}{6x^2},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\div  \\dfrac y {6x^2} &=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac 3 x \\cdot  \\dfrac {6x^2} y &=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {18x^2} {xy}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {18x^\\cancel{2}} {\\cancel{x}y}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {18x} {y}&\t\t\n\t}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $x$ in the numerator and denominator." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068598 :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {1+b}{5b^2} =y ,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $x \\div y,$ i.e., \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{a}^{2}(1+b)}{3b} & ÷\\frac{1+b}{5{b}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\div  \\dfrac{1+b}{5b^2}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^2(1+b)} {3b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^2} {1+b}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^2\\cancel{(1+b)}} {3\\cancel{b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {5b^{\\cancel{2}}} {\\cancel{1+b}}&=\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {10a^2b} {3}  \n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068764 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-64ed62a5-f26a-57d4-b199-8c8c51b737ac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068605 :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  =x$ and $\\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}=y,$ then what is $x \\div y?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  &\\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2},\n\t}\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\div  \\dfrac {2a} {14b^2}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a^3 - 10a} {7b}  \\cdot \\dfrac {14b^2} {2a}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {2a(a^2 - 5)} {7b}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot 7b} {2a}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {\\cancel{2a}(a^2 - 5)} {\\cancel{7b}}  \\cdot  \\dfrac {2b \\cdot \\cancel{7b}} {\\cancel{2a}}&=\\\n\t\t\\dfrac {2b(a^2 - 5)} 1 &=\\\n\t\t2a^2b - 10b &\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069503 :question/problem "$\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5b}{7}÷\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{5b}{7}\\cdot \\frac{a}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5ab}{14} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068188 :answer-field/correct 17592186068191 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-4dcf3cdc-27a5-54d1-8c1d-7131e7d4a1a0" :question/id #uuid "4dcf3cdc-27a5-54d1-8c1d-7131e7d4a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-113798" :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{5u}÷\\frac{3}{7v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5u}÷\\frac{3}{7v},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5u}÷\\frac{3}{7v} & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{5u}\\cdot \\frac{7v}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{14v}{15u} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa25d937-0cb4-59d9-82ac-b31ee7968be2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa25d937-0cb4-59d9-82ac-b31ee7968be2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa25d937-0cb4-59d9-82ac-b31ee7968be2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ac4b9935-1677-51f7-bfe7-8f40cf53d972" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac4b9935-1677-51f7-bfe7-8f40cf53d972" "answer-89b64097-1ab5-5998-aca4-a169f7923341" "answer-03b9ac09-8a0b-5e19-8eed-d5880937da6a" "answer-9bcefeb9-a1e2-5ee5-bae1-918c0d6a6ac9" "answer-29e6cb3e-b4ac-5e03-989e-fb0e1ead58f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac4b9935-1677-51f7-bfe7-8f40cf53d972" :answer/id #uuid "ac4b9935-1677-51f7-bfe7-8f40cf53d972" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14v}{15u}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89b64097-1ab5-5998-aca4-a169f7923341" :answer/id #uuid "89b64097-1ab5-5998-aca4-a169f7923341" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14}{15uv}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03b9ac09-8a0b-5e19-8eed-d5880937da6a" :answer/id #uuid "03b9ac09-8a0b-5e19-8eed-d5880937da6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15u}{14v}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bcefeb9-a1e2-5ee5-bae1-918c0d6a6ac9" :answer/id #uuid "9bcefeb9-a1e2-5ee5-bae1-918c0d6a6ac9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4v}{5u}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29e6cb3e-b4ac-5e03-989e-fb0e1ead58f1" :answer/id #uuid "29e6cb3e-b4ac-5e03-989e-fb0e1ead58f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{35uv}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069501 :question/problem "$\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3xz}{4}÷\\frac{2z}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{3xz}{4}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2z} & = \\\\ \\frac{15xz}{8z} & = \\\\ \\frac{15x\\cancel{z}}{8\\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{15x}{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the final step, we canceled out a common factor of $z$ in the numerator and denominator." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068176 :answer-field/correct 17592186068177 :answer-field/choices ["answer-504e0f2a-072f-5b1b-b073-c5b324045add"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-504e0f2a-072f-5b1b-b073-c5b324045add" :answer/id #uuid "504e0f2a-072f-5b1b-b073-c5b324045add" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6x{z}^{2}}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233189 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ebb594e-17b9-5253-8215-8996f4a7ec24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ebb594e-17b9-5253-8215-8996f4a7ec24" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ebb594e-17b9-5253-8215-8996f4a7ec24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d9cc3496-9375-504d-a3d2-06b507e752a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9cc3496-9375-504d-a3d2-06b507e752a3" "answer-ca928d1f-961b-583d-a769-be5f671d1b2a" "answer-620efcf8-fe45-5654-a7f3-88db5fb39541" "answer-c9dc84d0-2123-5c46-be45-9dca7733c77a" "answer-fb1bc848-a943-502b-beea-11031d924356"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9cc3496-9375-504d-a3d2-06b507e752a3" :answer/id #uuid "d9cc3496-9375-504d-a3d2-06b507e752a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14w}{3(w+2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca928d1f-961b-583d-a769-be5f671d1b2a" :answer/id #uuid "ca928d1f-961b-583d-a769-be5f671d1b2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{14{w}^{2}}{w+2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-620efcf8-fe45-5654-a7f3-88db5fb39541" :answer/id #uuid "620efcf8-fe45-5654-a7f3-88db5fb39541" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3(w+2)}{7{w}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9dc84d0-2123-5c46-be45-9dca7733c77a" :answer/id #uuid "c9dc84d0-2123-5c46-be45-9dca7733c77a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7w}{3(w+2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb1bc848-a943-502b-beea-11031d924356" :answer/id #uuid "fb1bc848-a943-502b-beea-11031d924356" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{w+2}{14}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069277 :question/problem "$\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}÷\\frac{36b}{20} & = \\\\ \\frac{12{b}^{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{20}{36b} & = \\\\ \\frac{12b\\cdot b}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 4}{12b\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12b}\\cdot b}{\\cancel{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{5}\\cdot 4}{\\cancel{12b}\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{4b}{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186066832 :answer-field/correct 17592186066836 :answer-field/choices ["answer-4dfe5412-1f1d-58e4-94f8-bff7c885cdee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dfe5412-1f1d-58e4-94f8-bff7c885cdee" :answer/id #uuid "4dfe5412-1f1d-58e4-94f8-bff7c885cdee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{b}^{2}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-64ed62a5-f26a-57d4-b199-8c8c51b737ac" :question/id #uuid "64ed62a5-f26a-57d4-b199-8c8c51b737ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-30011" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}=a$ and $\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{x-5}=b,$ what is $a÷b?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\na÷b=\\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}÷\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{x-5},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}÷\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{x-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-64}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{(x-8)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-8)(x+8)}{x-5}\\cdot \\frac{x-5}{(x-8)(x-8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(x-8)}(x+8)}{\\cancel{x-5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{x-5}}{\\cancel{(x-8)}(x-8)} & = \\\\ (x+8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{x+8}{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03a34a35-604f-5749-8973-20d9e084ced6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03a34a35-604f-5749-8973-20d9e084ced6" :answer-field/id #uuid "03a34a35-604f-5749-8973-20d9e084ced6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fec52fe6-cb5d-5c54-b138-3ee0039ec4da" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9718652-7a2a-51d9-93d2-a20b22cde1b5" "answer-aafbf437-5412-58dc-86e3-6f4bc151f85e" "answer-fec52fe6-cb5d-5c54-b138-3ee0039ec4da" "answer-b1b02b83-478f-5565-a598-0b24437db7e6" "answer-24240d5d-9699-58fb-8663-ef448a1296b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9718652-7a2a-51d9-93d2-a20b22cde1b5" :answer/id #uuid "e9718652-7a2a-51d9-93d2-a20b22cde1b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aafbf437-5412-58dc-86e3-6f4bc151f85e" :answer/id #uuid "aafbf437-5412-58dc-86e3-6f4bc151f85e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fec52fe6-cb5d-5c54-b138-3ee0039ec4da" :answer/id #uuid "fec52fe6-cb5d-5c54-b138-3ee0039ec4da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+8}{x-8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1b02b83-478f-5565-a598-0b24437db7e6" :answer/id #uuid "b1b02b83-478f-5565-a598-0b24437db7e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-5}{x+8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24240d5d-9699-58fb-8663-ef448a1296b2" :answer/id #uuid "24240d5d-9699-58fb-8663-ef448a1296b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186069973 :tutorial/content "To divide one rational expression by another, we multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. Just like with fractions!\n\nFor example, let's divide $x$ by $\\dfrac{3}{y}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx \\div \\dfrac{3}{y}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor. The divisor is $\\dfrac{3}{y},$ and to get the reciprocal, we just flip the fraction upside down: $\\dfrac{y}{3}.$ Multiplying by the reciprocal of the divisor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x÷\\frac{3}{y} & = \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & = \\\\ \\frac{xy}{3} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples!" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068967 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083907 :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $18$ and a third term equal to $2.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=18$ and ${a}_{3}=2.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{2}{18} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{(\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186082373 :answer-field/correct 17592186082374 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234230 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ed996d10-640b-5a4b-971a-4d70ea88d425"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ed996d10-640b-5a4b-971a-4d70ea88d425" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed996d10-640b-5a4b-971a-4d70ea88d425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b7e6727-bee6-581b-b90f-15564547ab7f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a8016a8-51b3-5faa-84af-ae76ac8a14e0" "answer-8b7e6727-bee6-581b-b90f-15564547ab7f" "answer-08a0745f-afdb-5fd5-896c-aecc342af1a1" "answer-f5bc4753-d8a5-5d2c-9d61-78c209c31473" "answer-c7ae6245-2f0d-51cd-8955-540a0d399231"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a8016a8-51b3-5faa-84af-ae76ac8a14e0" :answer/id #uuid "7a8016a8-51b3-5faa-84af-ae76ac8a14e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "107" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b7e6727-bee6-581b-b90f-15564547ab7f" :answer/id #uuid "8b7e6727-bee6-581b-b90f-15564547ab7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08a0745f-afdb-5fd5-896c-aecc342af1a1" :answer/id #uuid "08a0745f-afdb-5fd5-896c-aecc342af1a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5bc4753-d8a5-5d2c-9d61-78c209c31473" :answer/id #uuid "f5bc4753-d8a5-5d2c-9d61-78c209c31473" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7ae6245-2f0d-51cd-8955-540a0d399231" :answer/id #uuid "c7ae6245-2f0d-51cd-8955-540a0d399231" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083277 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e" :question/id #uuid "8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353118" :question/problem "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n-10-2-\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}-⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term ${a}_{1}=-10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-2)}{(-10)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-377f3da4-4d13-5ed9-a0c5-00b4eaa5cff5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-377f3da4-4d13-5ed9-a0c5-00b4eaa5cff5" :answer-field/id #uuid "377f3da4-4d13-5ed9-a0c5-00b4eaa5cff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd" :answer/id #uuid "98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b0043402-5216-56a5-82e2-182b3eaa6817" "question-b2fe4d85-a3ec-5968-ad10-7f316b6ab6b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0043402-5216-56a5-82e2-182b3eaa6817" :question/id #uuid "b0043402-5216-56a5-82e2-182b3eaa6817" :question/math-academy-id "q-353145" :question/problem "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n2.5+1.5+0.9+0.54+⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=2.5$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{1.5}{2.5}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.5}{1-0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.5}{0.4} \\\\  & =6.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3dfb5c0b-4f43-521c-adf9-750f8155535e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3dfb5c0b-4f43-521c-adf9-750f8155535e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3dfb5c0b-4f43-521c-adf9-750f8155535e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-387e8a10-560d-5d39-9e76-fc3b36e75ef1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-387e8a10-560d-5d39-9e76-fc3b36e75ef1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-387e8a10-560d-5d39-9e76-fc3b36e75ef1" :answer/id #uuid "387e8a10-560d-5d39-9e76-fc3b36e75ef1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b2fe4d85-a3ec-5968-ad10-7f316b6ab6b7" :question/id #uuid "b2fe4d85-a3ec-5968-ad10-7f316b6ab6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-353150" :question/problem "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n9.6-0.96+0.096-0.0096+⋯.\n$$\n\n Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=9.6$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-0.96}{9.6}=-0.1.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9.6}{1-(-0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9.6}{1+0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9.6}{1.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{960}{110} \\\\  & =\\frac{96}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28af4213-b2b7-5593-a629-18a8bcae5ad5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28af4213-b2b7-5593-a629-18a8bcae5ad5" :answer-field/id #uuid "28af4213-b2b7-5593-a629-18a8bcae5ad5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7bd96e13-17b0-50fe-8699-6fd760a67ac4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7bd96e13-17b0-50fe-8699-6fd760a67ac4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bd96e13-17b0-50fe-8699-6fd760a67ac4" :answer/id #uuid "7bd96e13-17b0-50fe-8699-6fd760a67ac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{96}{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5e90c252-d6d1-5d91-b294-f196d632abf7" :activity/id #uuid "5e90c252-d6d1-5d91-b294-f196d632abf7" :activity/title "Finding the Sum of an Infinite Geometric Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049404 :activity/course 17592186046645 :activity/steps ["step-a4dc42cc-fe3e-5bde-932d-e2411dc64c3b" "step-bdaa0d32-b906-59e0-aef8-a329efe98651" "step-8a955af1-f546-5512-be6c-5091a7427f58" "step-2523b52f-6ffd-5128-a11b-bfb54528d1ee" "step-e1c4fdaf-d25c-5b24-896e-fae19bcc0b58"] :activity/first-step "step-a4dc42cc-fe3e-5bde-932d-e2411dc64c3b" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a4dc42cc-fe3e-5bde-932d-e2411dc64c3b" :step/id #uuid "a4dc42cc-fe3e-5bde-932d-e2411dc64c3b" :step/content 17592186083907 :step/next "step-bdaa0d32-b906-59e0-aef8-a329efe98651" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bdaa0d32-b906-59e0-aef8-a329efe98651" :step/id #uuid "bdaa0d32-b906-59e0-aef8-a329efe98651" :step/content 17592186234230 :step/next "step-8a955af1-f546-5512-be6c-5091a7427f58" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8a955af1-f546-5512-be6c-5091a7427f58" :step/id #uuid "8a955af1-f546-5512-be6c-5091a7427f58" :step/content "question-8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e" :step/next "step-2523b52f-6ffd-5128-a11b-bfb54528d1ee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2523b52f-6ffd-5128-a11b-bfb54528d1ee" :step/id #uuid "2523b52f-6ffd-5128-a11b-bfb54528d1ee" :step/content "question-b0043402-5216-56a5-82e2-182b3eaa6817" :step/next "step-e1c4fdaf-d25c-5b24-896e-fae19bcc0b58" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e1c4fdaf-d25c-5b24-896e-fae19bcc0b58" :step/id #uuid "e1c4fdaf-d25c-5b24-896e-fae19bcc0b58" :step/content "question-b2fe4d85-a3ec-5968-ad10-7f316b6ab6b7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186332939 :question/worked-solution "First, note that $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{2})$ means the same thing as ${}_{15}{C}_{2}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=15$ and $r=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{15}{C}_{2}\\text{ } & =\\frac{15!}{(15-2)!2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{13!\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot \\cancel{13!}}{\\cancel{13!}\\cdot 2!} \\\\  & =105.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-209c7809-41c8-59af-ad52-c76253fbc621" :activity/id #uuid "209c7809-41c8-59af-ad52-c76253fbc621" :activity/title "Combinations" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049434 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-1991a9bc-4865-5adb-8f1b-8040622e710d" "step-e0a47519-8739-58a8-b095-6f1943584050" "step-0ff90f75-a9bd-5dc3-8d60-b987f3c76024"] :activity/first-step "step-1991a9bc-4865-5adb-8f1b-8040622e710d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1991a9bc-4865-5adb-8f1b-8040622e710d" :step/id #uuid "1991a9bc-4865-5adb-8f1b-8040622e710d" :step/content 17592186083574 :step/next "step-e0a47519-8739-58a8-b095-6f1943584050" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e0a47519-8739-58a8-b095-6f1943584050" :step/id #uuid "e0a47519-8739-58a8-b095-6f1943584050" :step/content 17592186332939 :step/next "step-0ff90f75-a9bd-5dc3-8d60-b987f3c76024" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0ff90f75-a9bd-5dc3-8d60-b987f3c76024" :step/id #uuid "0ff90f75-a9bd-5dc3-8d60-b987f3c76024" :step/content 17592186354369 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179677 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa31a49a-8ce4-5ed7-acd6-a1443ddcc296"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179676 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-59e76c83-715a-52bc-a7bd-8ad37145a622" "question-80763a51-c6ab-5b5d-83cb-358601dd22aa" "question-c384ade7-8329-5b20-a7ce-55cc1c6684d1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-59e76c83-715a-52bc-a7bd-8ad37145a622" :question/id #uuid "59e76c83-715a-52bc-a7bd-8ad37145a622" :question/math-academy-id "q-83971" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$  infected with a certain virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=2P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus started spreading. If only one person was infected initially, how many people will have been infected after $5$ days?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =2P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(200\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(200\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{200\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-2(0)}} & =1 \\\\ 1+C & =200\\,000 \\\\ C & =199\\,999\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{200\\,000}{1+199\\,999{e}^{-2t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=5$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =\\frac{200\\,000}{1+199\\,999{e}^{-2\\cdot 5}} \\\\  & ≈19\\,841.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $5$ days, approximately $19\\,841$ people have been infected." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8a04f500-6386-50b3-90bc-dc19f72347e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a04f500-6386-50b3-90bc-dc19f72347e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a04f500-6386-50b3-90bc-dc19f72347e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-df1451ad-15fb-555b-96d8-756c3d46621e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df1451ad-15fb-555b-96d8-756c3d46621e" "answer-7b129aba-1e60-5571-b939-cfee40621ab9" "answer-447be6ec-1715-5079-9b25-3bdc92bb6ad0" "answer-43ce7a4e-a22b-5aa7-8fe5-e882ab1347b5" "answer-969e13f3-1d67-58f7-bd3f-d60dbd798efa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df1451ad-15fb-555b-96d8-756c3d46621e" :answer/id #uuid "df1451ad-15fb-555b-96d8-756c3d46621e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19\\,841" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b129aba-1e60-5571-b939-cfee40621ab9" :answer/id #uuid "7b129aba-1e60-5571-b939-cfee40621ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "515" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-447be6ec-1715-5079-9b25-3bdc92bb6ad0" :answer/id #uuid "447be6ec-1715-5079-9b25-3bdc92bb6ad0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "73\\,172" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43ce7a4e-a22b-5aa7-8fe5-e882ab1347b5" :answer/id #uuid "43ce7a4e-a22b-5aa7-8fe5-e882ab1347b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "87" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-969e13f3-1d67-58f7-bd3f-d60dbd798efa" :answer/id #uuid "969e13f3-1d67-58f7-bd3f-d60dbd798efa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,425" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-80763a51-c6ab-5b5d-83cb-358601dd22aa" :question/id #uuid "80763a51-c6ab-5b5d-83cb-358601dd22aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120240" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ with food poisoning in an small town follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.15P-0.000\\,03{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the first reported cases. If there were $100$ cases initially, how many people with food poisoning there are after $4$ weeks?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.15P-0.000\\,03{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,03P(5\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,03,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5\\,000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(5\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,03)t}}=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.15t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =100 \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.15(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =50 \\\\ C & =49\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-0.15t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=4$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4) & =\\frac{5\\,000}{1+49{e}^{-0.15(4)}} \\\\  & ≈179.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $4$ weeks, there will be approximately $179$ people with food poisoning." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eddb9464-ca92-530f-b8d3-62388de749ee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eddb9464-ca92-530f-b8d3-62388de749ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "eddb9464-ca92-530f-b8d3-62388de749ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4d58b01f-da49-59b6-926d-d0d2cff7e680" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f46432d2-b7c1-5512-96c3-06133024f2ca" "answer-0f7e4caa-e1da-5f45-921e-9b9712388275" "answer-8f3e4d59-1a5f-5d73-a41d-09dcaa86c1b8" "answer-4d58b01f-da49-59b6-926d-d0d2cff7e680" "answer-e9f99c67-8f87-59fe-96cb-75f1084c69fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f46432d2-b7c1-5512-96c3-06133024f2ca" :answer/id #uuid "f46432d2-b7c1-5512-96c3-06133024f2ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "116" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f7e4caa-e1da-5f45-921e-9b9712388275" :answer/id #uuid "0f7e4caa-e1da-5f45-921e-9b9712388275" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "149" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f3e4d59-1a5f-5d73-a41d-09dcaa86c1b8" :answer/id #uuid "8f3e4d59-1a5f-5d73-a41d-09dcaa86c1b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d58b01f-da49-59b6-926d-d0d2cff7e680" :answer/id #uuid "4d58b01f-da49-59b6-926d-d0d2cff7e680" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "179" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9f99c67-8f87-59fe-96cb-75f1084c69fd" :answer/id #uuid "e9f99c67-8f87-59fe-96cb-75f1084c69fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa31a49a-8ce4-5ed7-acd6-a1443ddcc296" :question/id #uuid "aa31a49a-8ce4-5ed7-acd6-a1443ddcc296" :question/math-academy-id "q-353252" :question/problem "The growth rate of the rabbit population $P(t)$ in a wildlife preserve is jointly proportional to the rabbit population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the preserve’s carrying capacity and the rabbit population.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.0025$ and the carrying capacity is $1200,$ find an expression for the rabbit population after $t$ years, given that there were $200$ rabbits in the preserve initially.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"right\" rowspan=\"2\">\n      $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$\n    </td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      $1200$\n    </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\">\n      $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}}\n    </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Since the rabbit population’s growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the rabbit population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the rabbit population, the rabbit population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0025,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1200.$ Therefore, the rabbit population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0025\\,P(1200-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-(1200\\cdot 0.0025)t}}=\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=200$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1200}{1+C{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}} & =200 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1200}{200} \\\\ C & =6-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1200}{1+5{e}^{-3t}}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ea0a3a4-ab47-57c2-94ec-2bcd3b16dd3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ea0a3a4-ab47-57c2-94ec-2bcd3b16dd3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ea0a3a4-ab47-57c2-94ec-2bcd3b16dd3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ea8eee30-c4b3-5768-b0a1-f0b46ef787ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea8eee30-c4b3-5768-b0a1-f0b46ef787ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea8eee30-c4b3-5768-b0a1-f0b46ef787ca" :answer/id #uuid "ea8eee30-c4b3-5768-b0a1-f0b46ef787ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5e^{-3t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c384ade7-8329-5b20-a7ce-55cc1c6684d1" :question/id #uuid "c384ade7-8329-5b20-a7ce-55cc1c6684d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-353266" :question/problem "The growth rate of the number of households $P(t)$ participating in a neighborhood composting program is jointly proportional to the number of participating households at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the program’s carrying capacity and the number of participating households. The constant of proportionality is $0.0005$ and the carrying capacity is $800.$\n\nHow many households will be participating after $7$ years given that there were $100$ households participating initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $7$ years, there will be approximately \n{{field-1}} households participating in the program." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of households participating grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of households participating at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of households participating, the number of households participating follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.0005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.0005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0.4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0.4(0)}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =8 \\\\ C & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-0.4t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-0.4(7)}} \\\\  & ≈561.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $7$ years, there will be approximately $561$ households participating in the program." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34bc565b-3149-51bd-8b80-246eddcdca66"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34bc565b-3149-51bd-8b80-246eddcdca66" :answer-field/id #uuid "34bc565b-3149-51bd-8b80-246eddcdca66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ae9d1ff5-6c10-59e0-b65c-31cf4ae54efa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae9d1ff5-6c10-59e0-b65c-31cf4ae54efa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae9d1ff5-6c10-59e0-b65c-31cf4ae54efa" :answer/id #uuid "ae9d1ff5-6c10-59e0-b65c-31cf4ae54efa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "561" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-995e9bad-2373-581b-bf84-a4c6b91b00ca" :activity/id #uuid "995e9bad-2373-581b-bf84-a4c6b91b00ca" :activity/title "Solving the Logistic Growth Equation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 175